X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Eskenazi School of Art": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception-for-the-photographic", "Architecture + Design": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception-for-the-photographic", "IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception-for-the-photographic", "Eskenazi Museum of Art": "https://events.iu.edu/artmuseum/event/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception-for-the-photographic"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 11": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception-for-the-photographic"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:Exhibition Opening Reception for The Photographic Reflex: The Lee 
 Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection
DTSTART:20260910T200000Z
DTEND:20260910T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20260504T145108Z
UID:20260910T200000Z-2287041@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260910T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,2640/31057_2022.281_view_1.rev.1777558929.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Functions, festivals, and celebratio
 ns
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, September 10\, 20264–6 p.m. Opening reception  Ho
 rs d'oeuvres and cash bar (4–6 p.m.)  First floor atrium  About The Phot
 ographic Reflex: The Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection (https://
 artmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/upcoming/_feature-exhibitions/2026-08-29
 -photographic-reflex-marks-deprez.html):  Spanning more than 150 years\, T
 he Photographic Reflex celebrates a transformative gift to the IU Eskenazi
  Museum of Art from collectors Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. The exhibi
 tion features more than one hundred photographs by eighty artists\, highli
 ghting the breadth\, power\, and enduring impact of photographic expressio
 n.  Photographs dating from 1856 to 2017 reveal photography's dual role as
  both a tool for documentation and a vehicle for artistic exploration. Org
 anized into five thematic sections—Play of Light\, Industrial Eye\, Phot
 ographic Figure\, Witness to Conflict\, and Impressed by Nature's Hand—t
 he exhibition examines how photographers across disciplines have communica
 ted ideas\, recorded history\, and shaped visual culture.  The exhibition 
 includes works by many of the most influential figures in the medium\, fro
 m Berenice Abbott\, Alfred Stieglitz\, Edward Steichen\, and Henri Cartier
 -Bresson to contemporary artists such as Edward Burtynsky\, Abelardo Morel
 l\, Susan Derges\, and Vik Muniz.  A richly illustrated exhibition catalog
 ue\, featuring an interview with the collectors\, will accompany The Photo
 graphic Reflex and will be available for purchase through the museum's New
 kirk Café and Gift Shop.
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
LAST-MODIFIED:20260608T154901Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283988-exhibition-opening-reception
 -for-the-photographic
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Thursday\, September 10\, 2026</st
 rong></p><p><strong>4–6 p.m. Opening reception</strong></p><p>  Hors d'o
 euvres and cash bar (4–6 p.m.)</p><p>  First floor atrium</p><p>  About 
 <i><a href="https://artmuseum.indiana.edu/exhibitions/upcoming/_feature-ex
 hibitions/2026-08-29-photographic-reflex-marks-deprez.html">The Photograph
 ic Reflex: The Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. Collection</a></i>:</p><p>
   Spanning more than 150 years\, <i>The Photographic Reflex</i> celebrates
  a transformative gift to the IU Eskenazi Museum of Art from collectors Le
 e Marks and John C. DePrez Jr. The exhibition features more than one hundr
 ed photographs by eighty artists\, highlighting the breadth\, power\, and 
 enduring impact of photographic expression.</p><p>  Photographs dating fro
 m 1856 to 2017 reveal photography's dual role as both a tool for documenta
 tion and a vehicle for artistic exploration. Organized into five thematic 
 sections—<i>Play of Light</i>\, <i>Industrial Eye</i>\, <i>Photographic 
 Figure</i>\, <i>Witness to Conflict</i>\, and <i>Impressed by Nature's Han
 d</i>—the exhibition examines how photographers across disciplines have 
 communicated ideas\, recorded history\, and shaped visual culture.</p><p> 
  The exhibition includes works by many of the most influential figures in 
 the medium\, from Berenice Abbott\, Alfred Stieglitz\, Edward Steichen\, a
 nd Henri Cartier-Bresson to contemporary artists such as Edward Burtynsky\
 , Abelardo Morell\, Susan Derges\, and Vik Muniz.</p><p>  A richly illustr
 ated exhibition catalogue\, featuring an interview with the collectors\, w
 ill accompany <i>The Photographic Reflex</i> and will be available for pur
 chase through the museum's Newkirk Café and Gift Shop.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Emily Zarse<br />\n  Public Experiences Ma
 nager<br />\n  eczarse@iu.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2287041
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,3200\,2640/31057_2022.281_view_1.rev.1777558
 929.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Susan Derges (English\, b. 1955). <strong>Untitl
 ed (eye no. 7)</strong>\, 1991. Gelatin silver print\, 11 7/16 × 14 1/8 i
 n. (29.1 × 35.9 cm). Gift of Lee Marks and John C. DePrez Jr.\, Shelbyvil
