BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2391953-chornobyl-never-ends-40-years-later"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Chornobyl Never Ends: 40 Years Later
DTSTART:20260911T140000Z
DTEND:20260911T150000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153754Z
UID:20260911T140000Z-2402269@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260911T140000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/182/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1996,1175/32147_Chornobyl40-cover.rev.1787579674.jpg
DESCRIPTION:On April 26\, 1986\, the fourth reactor of the Chornobyl Nucle
 ar Power Plant exploded\, marking one of the largest technological and env
 ironmental disasters of the twentieth century—a transnational\, epidemio
 logical\, environmental\, epistemological\, and historiographical rupture.
   The event had far-reaching consequences for global health policy\, nucle
 ar energy development\, geopolitical power structures\, and our understand
 ing of knowledge\, technology\, and the environment. Yet Chornobyl never e
 nds.  Chornobyl Never Ends returns to the discourse and experience of Chor
 nobyl forty years after the disaster\, looking beyond both the collapse of
  the Soviet Union and the post-Soviet period to consider its continuing si
 gnificance in the wake of four years of Russia's full-scale invasion of Uk
 raine.  Oscillating between past\, present\, and future\, the symposium as
 ks what Chornobyl continues to mean—and what it makes visible—four dec
 ades later. How has the disaster shaped our understanding of technology\, 
 ecology\, risk\, memory\, and political power? How have its meanings chang
 ed across different historical moments and political contexts? And what ca
 n the history of Chornobyl tell us about the uncertain nuclear futures ahe
 ad?  September 11\, 2026  10 a.m. – Keynote lecture:  "Communist Technol
 ogy\, Rogue Operators\, and a Dysfunctional Industry: Was Chernobyl an Acc
 ident Waiting to Happen?"  Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commissio
 n / Department of Science\, Technology and Society\, Virginia Tech)  Lectu
 re abstract:  The world's worst catastrophe at a nuclear facility\, the 19
 86 Chornobyl disaster\, has alternately been blamed on a faulty reactor de
 sign\, incompetent reactor personnel\, or a deeply flawed economic system.
  This talk traces the extended "blame game" that ensued the accident and i
 ts mitigation and shows that neither of these factors alone caused the dis
 aster. Rather\, it argues\, despite the general success of Soviet technolo
 gy\, the country's outstanding engineering education\, and a dynamic manag
 erial culture\, Chornobyl was an accident of history: both deeply Soviet\,
  and at the same time possible elsewhere.  Speaker's bio:  Sonja Schmid is
  a professor in the Department of Science\, Technology\, and Society at Vi
 rginia Tech (currently on leave)\, and the Historian at the U.S. Nuclear R
 egulatory Commission. Her research is located at the interface of national
  energy policies\, technological choices\, and nonproliferation concerns. 
 Her first book\, Producing Power: The Pre‑Chernobyl History of the Sovie
 t Nuclear Industry\, was published by MIT Press in 2015. Her next project 
 focused on the challenges of globalizing nuclear emergency response\, and 
 was supported by an NSF CAREER Award. In addition to teaching for and co-d
 irecting the STS graduate program in Northern Virginia\, Dr. Schmid develo
 ped and supports a graduate certificate\, "Nuclear Science\, Technology\, 
 and Policy\," which is supported by the nuclear engineering program and th
 e policy school.  Location: GISB 1060  11:30 a.m. – Film program:  "Nucl
 ear Cinema: Imagining Anxiety\, Trauma\, and War"  Juggernaut (1968\, dir.
  Eugene Boyko) — 27 min  Alive Yet (1992\, dir. Heorhii Shkliarevskyi) 
 — 28 min  Chornobyl 22 (2023\, dir. Oleksiy Radynski) — 22 min  Locati
 on: Wells Library Room 048 (IU Libraries Moving Image Archive Screening Ro
 om)  2:30 p.m. – Panel Discussion:  "Media\, Environment\, and Nuclear S
 ecurity after Chornobyl"  Participants:  ● Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear Re
 gulatory Commission / Virginia Tech)  ● Andy Bruno (IU)  ● Stanislav M
 enzelevskyi (IU)  Moderation: Sarah Phillips (IU)  Location: GISB 4067  Co
 sponsored by The Byrnes Institute (REEI)\, the Ukrainian Studies Organizat
 ion\, the Moving Images Archive\, the Department of History\, and the Cohe
 n Chair in Russian History.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193754Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2391953-chornobyl-never-ends-40-year
 s-later
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  On April 26\, 1986\, the fourth reactor 
 of the Chornobyl Nuclear Power Plant exploded\, marking one of the largest
  technological and environmental disasters of the twentieth century—a tr
 ansnational\, epidemiological\, environmental\, epistemological\, and hist
 oriographical rupture.</p><p>  The event had far-reaching consequences for
  global health policy\, nuclear energy development\, geopolitical power st
 ructures\, and our understanding of knowledge\, technology\, and the envir
 onment. Yet Chornobyl never ends.</p><p>  Chornobyl Never Ends returns to 
 the discourse and experience of Chornobyl forty years after the disaster\,
  looking beyond both the collapse of the Soviet Union and the post-Soviet 
 period to consider its continuing significance in the wake of four years o
 f Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine.</p><p>  Oscillating between pas
 t\, present\, and future\, the symposium asks what Chornobyl continues to 
 mean—and what it makes visible—four decades later. How has the disaste
 r shaped our understanding of technology\, ecology\, risk\, memory\, and p
 olitical power? How have its meanings changed across different historical 
 moments and political contexts? And what can the history of Chornobyl tell
  us about the uncertain nuclear futures ahead?</p><p>  September 11\, 2026
 </p><p>  10 a.m. – Keynote lecture:</p><p>  "Communist Technology\, Rogu
 e Operators\, and a Dysfunctional Industry: Was Chernobyl an Accident Wait
 ing to Happen?"</p><p>  Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission /
  Department of Science\, Technology and Society\, Virginia Tech)</p><p>  L
 ecture abstract:</p><p>  The world's worst catastrophe at a nuclear facili
 ty\, the 1986 Chornobyl disaster\, has alternately been blamed on a faulty
  reactor design\, incompetent reactor personnel\, or a deeply flawed econo
 mic system. This talk traces the extended "blame game" that ensued the acc
 ident and its mitigation and shows that neither of these factors alone cau
 sed the disaster. Rather\, it argues\, despite the general success of Sovi
 et technology\, the country's outstanding engineering education\, and a dy
 namic managerial culture\, Chornobyl was an accident of history: both deep
 ly Soviet\, and at the same time possible elsewhere.</p><p>  Speaker's bio
 :</p><p>  Sonja Schmid is a professor in the Department of Science\, Techn
 ology\, and Society at Virginia Tech (currently on leave)\, and the Histor
 ian at the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission. Her research is located at 
 the interface of national energy policies\, technological choices\, and no
 nproliferation concerns. Her first book\, Producing Power: The Pre‑Chern
 obyl History of the Soviet Nuclear Industry\, was published by MIT Press i
 n 2015. Her next project focused on the challenges of globalizing nuclear 
 emergency response\, and was supported by an NSF CAREER Award. In addition
  to teaching for and co-directing the STS graduate program in Northern Vir
 ginia\, Dr. Schmid developed and supports a graduate certificate\, "Nuclea
 r Science\, Technology\, and Policy\," which is supported by the nuclear e
 ngineering program and the policy school.</p><p>  Location: GISB 1060</p><
 p><br />  11:30 a.m. – Film program:</p><p>  "Nuclear Cinema: Imagining 
 Anxiety\, Trauma\, and War"</p><p>  Juggernaut (1968\, dir. Eugene Boyko) 
 — 27 min</p><p>  Alive Yet (1992\, dir. Heorhii Shkliarevskyi) — 28 mi
 n</p><p>  Chornobyl 22 (2023\, dir. Oleksiy Radynski) — 22 min</p><p>  L
 ocation: Wells Library Room 048 (IU Libraries Moving Image Archive Screeni
 ng Room)</p><p>  2:30 p.m. – Panel Discussion:</p><p>  "Media\, Environm
 ent\, and Nuclear Security after Chornobyl"</p><p>  Participants:</p><p>  
 ● Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission / Virginia Tech)</p><
 p>  ● Andy Bruno (IU)</p><p>  ● Stanislav Menzelevskyi (IU)</p><p>  Mo
 deration: Sarah Phillips (IU)</p><p>  Location: GISB 4067</p><p>  Cosponso
 red by The Byrnes Institute (REEI)\, the Ukrainian Studies Organization\, 
 the Moving Images Archive\, the Department of History\, and the Cohen Chai
 r in Russian History.</p>
