SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Sue Hyon Bae
DTSTART:20260911T160000Z
DTEND:20260911T170000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153503Z
UID:20260911T160000Z-2402247@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260911T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/177/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,585,614/31807_dissertation_pic.rev.1786128275.png
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:IKS Fall 2026 Colloquium Series  Sue Hyon Bae  Postdoctoral Fe
 llow  Institute for Korean Studies  "Back to Nature: Traditional Medicine 
 and Cancer Cures"Friday\, September 1112:00-1:00pmGISB 2067 | 355 N Eagles
 on Ave  "South Korea has a long history of traditional medicine. Its found
 ational text\, Hŏ Chun's Tongŭi Pogam\, published under royal edict in 1
 613\, is popularly cited today\, and basic concepts adapted from tradition
 al medicine\, such as food as medicine and the superiority of local ingred
 ients\, are commonly practiced along with biomedicine. In this presentatio
 n\, I analyze memoirs and self-help books that promote traditional Korean 
 medicine (TKM) for the treatment of cancer. Written by diverse people\, so
 me practitioners of TKM and others patients\, they share a common commitme
 nt to spreading an ideology\, or a narrative about how illness is created 
 and cured.  Although the details such as what kind of natural cure and the
  degree of faith they place in biomedicine differ\, the TKM narratives ult
 imately argue that there is something wrong with the state of society. The
  TKM narratives also emphasize personal choice and control\, showing a uni
 que syncretization of modern and traditional medicine with neoliberal valu
 es. Many people\, disillusioned not only with modern biomedicine but with 
 modernity itself\, turn to alternatives just as they would shop for differ
 ent products in the belief they can buy health. What people blame for a di
 sease that seems to have appeared out of nowhere\, what they believe will 
 cure them\, and how traditional medicine is adapting itself to modernity e
 ven as it presents itself as a cure for modernity\, is highly revealing ab
 out contemporary South Korean attitudes towards health\, illness\, and mod
 ern life."  Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.
GEO:39.170011;-86.517418
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193503Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2375497-iks-colloquium-sue-hyon-bae
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Fall 2
 026 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br />  Sue Hyo
 n Bae</h6><p style="text-align: center\;">  Postdoctoral Fellow<br />  Ins
 titute for Korean Studies</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  "Back to Na
 ture: Traditional Medicine and Cancer Cures"</p><p style="text-align: cent
 er\;"><strong>Friday\, September 11</strong></p><p style="text-align: cent
 er\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p style="text-align: center\;"><sp
 an class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feature-
 calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none"><strong>GISB 2067 |
  355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br />  "South Korea has a long history 
 of traditional medicine. Its foundational text\, Hŏ Chun's Tongŭi Pogam\
 , published under royal edict in 1613\, is popularly cited today\, and bas
 ic concepts adapted from traditional medicine\, such as food as medicine a
 nd the superiority of local ingredients\, are commonly practiced along wit
 h biomedicine. In this presentation\, I analyze memoirs and self-help book
 s that promote traditional Korean medicine (TKM) for the treatment of canc
 er. Written by diverse people\, some practitioners of TKM and others patie
 nts\, they share a common commitment to spreading an ideology\, or a narra
 tive about how illness is created and cured.<br />  Although the details s
 uch as what kind of natural cure and the degree of faith they place in bio
 medicine differ\, the TKM narratives ultimately argue that there is someth
 ing wrong with the state of society. The TKM narratives also emphasize per
 sonal choice and control\, showing a unique syncretization of modern and t
 raditional medicine with neoliberal values. Many people\, disillusioned no
 t only with modern biomedicine but with modernity itself\, turn to alterna
 tives just as they would shop for different products in the belief they ca
 n buy health. What people blame for a disease that seems to have appeared 
 out of nowhere\, what they believe will cure them\, and how traditional me
 dicine is adapting itself to modernity even as it presents itself as a cur
 e for modernity\, is highly revealing about contemporary South Korean atti
 tudes towards health\, illness\, and modern life."<br /><br />  Open to th
 e public. A light lunch will be served.</span></p>
