BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2396935-american-foreign-policy-in-the-shadow-of-911"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:American Foreign Policy in the Shadow of 9/11
DTSTART:20260911T183000Z
DTEND:20260911T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153518Z
UID:20260911T183000Z-2402252@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260911T183000Z
CATEGORIES:Career development,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,T
 raining, non-credit classes, and learning opportunities
DESCRIPTION:Twenty-five years ago on 9/11\, al-Qaeda terrorists attacked t
 he United States\, inflicting a death toll greater than Japan's surprise a
 ttack on Pearl Harbor. The attacks delivered a profound shock to America's
  sense of security and place in the world and the international system. In
  response\, the United States undertook a far-reaching "War on Terror"—t
 he aftermath of which continues to influence our politics\, national secur
 ity\, and the global balance of power. Counterterrorism\, once a periphera
 l issue\, quickly became the dominant policy priority. Washington launched
  large-scale and prolonged military interventions in Afghanistan\, Iraq\, 
 and other crisis hotspots. Longstanding alliances were tested as new partn
 erships emerged. New forms of unmanned warfare were pioneered while nation
 al security agencies ballooned in size\, budgets\, and legal authorities. 
 In short\, 9/11 transformed American statecraft.  Join Professor of Practi
 ce Zachary Constantino for a moderated conversation with U.S. Ambassador (
 retired) Michael McKinley to unpack the wide-ranging impact the September 
 11 attacks had on American foreign policy. This will be an afternoon of re
 flection\, assessing two decades of U.S. strategy and the legacy of those 
 policies. Ambassador McKinley served for thirty-seven years in the U.S. Fo
 reign Service\, including as Ambassador to Afghanistan\, Brazil\, Peru\, a
 nd Colombia. He also held senior diplomatic posts in Europe\, Africa\, and
  Washington\, DC. Ambassador McKinley's experience on the front lines of d
 iplomacy is extensive\, encompassing efforts to advance peace negotiations
  in Colombia and Afghanistan to end conflict as well as successfully negot
 iating agreements to promote U.S. investment and market access. He is curr
 ently a senior counselor at The Cohen Group and a non-resident senior advi
 sor with the Center for Strategic and International Studies. Ambassador Mc
 Kinley is the author of numerous articles on foreign policy that have appe
 ared in Foreign Affairs\, Politico\, The&#160\;Washington Post\, and other
  publications.  The IU Chapter of the Alexander Hamilton Society is co-spo
 nsoring this event.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193518Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2396935-american-foreign-policy-in-t
 he-shadow-of-911
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Twenty-five years ago on 9/11\, al-Qaeda
  terrorists attacked the United States\, inflicting a death toll greater t
 han Japan's surprise attack on Pearl Harbor. The attacks delivered a profo
 und shock to America's sense of security and place in the world and the in
 ternational system. In response\, the United States undertook a far-reachi
 ng "War on Terror"—the aftermath of which continues to influence our pol
 itics\, national security\, and the global balance of power. Counterterror
 ism\, once a peripheral issue\, quickly became the dominant policy priorit
 y. Washington launched large-scale and prolonged military interventions in
  Afghanistan\, Iraq\, and other crisis hotspots. Longstanding alliances we
 re tested as new partnerships emerged. New forms of unmanned warfare were 
 pioneered while national security agencies ballooned in size\, budgets\, a
 nd legal authorities. In short\, 9/11 transformed American statecraft.</p>
 <p>  Join Professor of Practice Zachary Constantino for a moderated conver
 sation with U.S. Ambassador (retired) Michael McKinley to unpack the wide-
 ranging impact the September 11 attacks had on American foreign policy. Th
 is will be an afternoon of reflection\, assessing two decades of U.S. stra
 tegy and the legacy of those policies. Ambassador McKinley served for thir
 ty-seven years in the U.S. Foreign Service\, including as Ambassador to Af
 ghanistan\, Brazil\, Peru\, and Colombia. He also held senior diplomatic p
 osts in Europe\, Africa\, and Washington\, DC. Ambassador McKinley's exper
 ience on the front lines of diplomacy is extensive\, encompassing efforts 
 to advance peace negotiations in Colombia and Afghanistan to end conflict 
 as well as successfully negotiating agreements to promote U.S. investment 
 and market access. He is currently a senior counselor at The Cohen Group a
 nd a non-resident senior advisor with the Center for Strategic and Interna
 tional Studies. Ambassador McKinley is the author of numerous articles on 
 foreign policy that have appeared in <em>Foreign Affairs</em>\, <em>Politi
 co</em>\, <em>The&#160\;Washington Post</em>\, and other publications.</p>
 <p>  The IU Chapter of the Alexander Hamilton Society is co-sponsoring thi
 s event.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Professor of Practice Zachary Constantino<
 br />\n  Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies<br />\n
   (<a href="mailto:zconstan@iu.edu">zconstan@iu.edu</a>)\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2402252
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Join a conversation with a former U.S. diplomat
  to reflect on the consequential impact the 9/11 terrorist attacks had on 
 American foreign policy.&#160\;&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:Academics|Fall Semester|Grad Events|In Person|Social and 
 Historical Sciences|Undergrad Events
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
END:VEVENT
