SUMMARY:Eiteljorg Artist In Residence Visiting Lecture: Carrie Moran McCle
 ary
DTSTART:20260916T170000Z
DTEND:20260916T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260818T132821Z
UID:20260916T170000Z-2385073@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260916T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,466,468/31958_Screenshot_2026-08-13_at_12.34.59PM.rev.178
 6639077.png
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Culture and languages,Functions, fes
 tivals, and celebrations,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:1–2 p.m. Artist talk\, Learn more about Carrie's artistic jo
 urney\, creative philosophy\, and evolving practice\, followed by an open 
 Q+A session\, Martin Commons\, second floor2–4 p.m. Enjoy hands-on artis
 t programming and a rare look at selected objects from the museum's collec
 tions.  Carrie Moran McCleary is a fashion designer\, beader\, and doll ma
 ker from the Little Shell Chippewa Tribe. She lives on the Crow Reservatio
 n in Montana. In 2016\, she founded Plains Soul to share her unique style 
 and artistry with a wider audience. Moran McCleary is known for Indigenizi
 ng everyday items\, transforming thrift-store finds and hand-me-downs into
  works of art that express her identity. Taught by her mothers to bead and
  sew\, she then developed her own style that infuses traditional practices
  with contemporary design.All of these activities are free and open to the
  public. This program is co-organized by the IU First Nations Educational 
 and Cultural Center and the Eskenazi Museum of Art\, with support from the
  Eiteljorg Museum of American Indians and Western Art.
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
LAST-MODIFIED:20260826T161725Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2381243-eiteljorg-artist-in-residenc
 e-visiting-lecture-carri
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="font-weight: 400\;"><strong>1–2 p
 .m.</strong> Artist talk\, Learn more about Carrie's artistic journey\, cr
 eative philosophy\, and evolving practice\, followed by an open Q+A sessio
 n\, Martin Commons\, second floor</p><p style="font-weight: 400\;"><strong
 >2–4 p.m.</strong> Enjoy hands-on artist programming and a rare look at 
 selected objects from the museum's collections.</p><p style="font-weight: 
 400\;">  Carrie Moran McCleary is a fashion designer\, beader\, and doll m
 aker from the Little Shell Chippewa Tribe. She lives on the Crow Reservati
 on in Montana. In 2016\, she founded Plains Soul to share her unique style
  and artistry with a wider audience. Moran McCleary is known for Indigeniz
 ing everyday items\, transforming thrift-store finds and hand-me-downs int
 o works of art that express her identity. Taught by her mothers to bead an
 d sew\, she then developed her own style that infuses traditional practice
 s with contemporary design.</p><div><p><span class="TextRun SCXW242918722 
 BCX4" lang="EN-US" xml:lang="EN-US" data-contrast="none"><span class="Norm
 alTextRun SCXW242918722 BCX4"><strong>All of these activities are free and
  open to the public. This program is co-organized by the IU First Nations 
 Educational and Cultural Center and the Eskenazi Museum of Art\, with supp
 ort from the Eiteljorg Museum of American Indians and Western Art.</strong
 ></span></span></p></div>
