BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2354119-home-away-from-home-student-life-on-campus"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:Home Away from Home: Student Life on Campus
DTSTART:20260918T130000Z
DTEND:20260918T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260717T152335Z
UID:20260907T130000Z-2354119@events.iu.edu__20260918
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1800,1800/31497_StudentLifeThumbnail_1.rev.1784316196.png
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:Spanning across four decades—1970s to the 2000s—items incl
 uding scrapbooks\, photographs\, student papers\, and handbooks demonstrat
 e life on and off campus. From decorating dorm rooms to chasing campus gho
 sts\, students have always found ways to build community and make new memo
 ries. This exhibit highlights the student experience and the multitude of 
 ways students found a sense of home\, identity and belonging at Indiana Un
 iversity.
GEO:39.171003;-86.516793
LAST-MODIFIED:20260717T192335Z
LOCATION:HERMAN B WELLS LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2354119-home-away-from-home-stud
 ent-life-on-campus
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Spanning across four decades—1970s to 
 the 2000s—items including scrapbooks\, photographs\, student papers\, an
 d handbooks demonstrate life on and off campus. From decorating dorm rooms
  to chasing campus ghosts\, students have always found ways to build commu
 nity and make new memories. This exhibit highlights the student experience
  and the multitude of ways students found a sense of home\, identity and b
 elonging at Indiana University.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>University Archives\, <a href="mailto:archives
 @iu.edu">archives@iu.edu</a>&#160\;</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2354119
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1800\,1800/31497_StudentLifeThumbnail_1.rev.
 1784316196.png
X-LIVEWHALE-REPEATS-UNTIL:2026-10-30 13:00:00
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>Going to college offers a variety of new freedoms\,
  excitements\, and difficulties as students make the transition away from 
 home. This exhibit features a selection of materials that represents the w
 ays first-year students made Indiana University their second home.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:exhibition|Hoosier Welcome|Indiana University Archives|IU
  History
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 343": "https://events.iu.edu/kelleyiub/event/2398507-assessing-the-effectiveness-of-teaching-strategies"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_343
X-SOURCE:IU Kelley School of Business
DTSTART:20260918T130000Z
DTEND:20260918T143000Z
LOCATION:HODGE HALL (KELLEY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS)
GEO:39.172366;-86.518375
SUMMARY:Assessing the Effectiveness of Teaching Strategies in Large Classes
DESCRIPTION:Presenter: Francesco Marchionne (Sr. Lecturer\, Business Econ
 omics)  At some point or other\, every teacher wonders about the effectiv
 eness of his or her pedagogical choices: did the learning activities actu
 ally make a difference or did students learn simply from repeated exposur
 e to the material? This question is perhaps even more pronounced in the l
 arge class setting\, where large enrollments make it difficult to track w
 hat students actually do.  In this first Kelley Teaching Seminar of the 2
 026-27 academic year\, Assurance of Learning Award winner Francesco March
 ionne will present his research\, which controls for the natural "learnin
 g curve bias" and provides real-time\, same-semester feedback that instru
 ctors can use to optimize instruction and maximize student learning\, eve
 n in large classes.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Presenter: Francesco Marchionne (Sr. Le
 cturer\, Business Economics)</p><p>  At some point or other\, every teach
 er wonders about the effectiveness of his or her pedagogical choices: did
  the learning activities actually make a difference or did students learn
  simply from repeated exposure to the material? This question is perhaps 
 even more pronounced in the large class setting\, where large enrollments
  make it difficult to track what students actually do.</p><p>  In this fi
 rst Kelley Teaching Seminar of the 2026-27 academic year\, Assurance of L
 earning Award winner Francesco Marchionne will present his research\, whi
 ch controls for the natural "learning curve bias" and provides real-time\
 , same-semester feedback that instructors can use to optimize instruction
  and maximize student learning\, even in large classes.</p>
UID:20260918T130000Z-2398507@events.iu.edu
DTSTAMP:20260827T101453Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/kelleyiub/event/2398507-assessing-the-effective
 ness-of-teaching-strategies
LAST-MODIFIED:20260827T144253Z
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2398507
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
END:VEVENT
