BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388023-tobias-center-research-seminar-prolonged-harm-and-le"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Vio
 lence
DTSTART:20260918T160000Z
DTEND:20260918T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153755Z
UID:20260918T160000Z-2402270@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260918T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/185/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1080,1350/32225_Khoury_Social_Media_Graphic.rev.178784035
 0.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series\
 , entitled "Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Violence\," features Rana Khour
 y\, Assistant Professor of Political Science at the University of Illinois
  Urbana-Champaign.Abstract: Most refugees live in protracted displacement 
 for years without achieving permanent rights. Civilians live through wars 
 themselves protracted. How do war's victims think and behave when circumst
 ances change? Conflict legacies scholarship conceptualizes violence as dis
 crete exposure and examines its effects on victims after political conditi
 ons have stabilized. These approaches overlook prolonged harm: cumulative\
 , mundane\, and ongoing hardship that persists beyond episodic violence an
 d shapes how individuals experience change itself. Prolonged harm cumulate
 s across multiple dimensions: psychological\, structural\, material\, and 
 emotional. A proposed conceptual framework captures how Syrian refugees na
 vigated two critical but highly distinct moments late in their displacemen
 t—natural disaster and regime change. Both activated and exposed the cum
 ulated harm of conflict victims. Findings suggest conflict research must a
 ccount for quotidian hardship that endogenously imprints conflict legacies
 . Differing methodological traditions can incorporate a prolonged harm fra
 mework.Lunch will be provided.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193755Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388023-tobias-center-research-semin
 ar-prolonged-harm-and-le
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center re
 search seminar series\, entitled "Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Violence\
 ," features Rana Khoury\, Assistant Professor of Political Science at the 
 University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign.</p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong> 
 Most refugees live in protracted displacement for years without achieving 
 permanent rights. Civilians live through wars themselves protracted. How d
 o war's victims think and behave when circumstances change? Conflict legac
 ies scholarship conceptualizes violence as discrete exposure and examines 
 its effects on victims after political conditions have stabilized. These a
 pproaches overlook prolonged harm: cumulative\, mundane\, and ongoing hard
 ship that persists beyond episodic violence and shapes how individuals exp
 erience change itself. Prolonged harm cumulates across multiple dimensions
 : psychological\, structural\, material\, and emotional. A proposed concep
 tual framework captures how Syrian refugees navigated two critical but hig
 hly distinct moments late in their displacement—natural disaster and reg
 ime change. Both activated and exposed the cumulated harm of conflict vict
 ims. Findings suggest conflict research must account for quotidian hardshi
 p that endogenously imprints conflict legacies. Differing methodological t
 raditions can incorporate a prolonged harm framework.</p><p><strong>Lunch 
 will be provided.</strong></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2402270
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/185/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1080\,1350/32225_Khoury_Social_Media_Graphic
 .rev.1787840350.png
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Event Flyer
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
END:VEVENT
