SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Alyssa Paredes
DTSTART:20260921T160000Z
DTEND:20260921T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153737Z
UID:20260921T160000Z-2402258@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260921T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,640,426/12533_globe-1130870_640.rev.1659031295.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Alyssa Paredes is an anthropologist with interests in ind
 ustrial agriculture\, transnational supply chains\, and environmental liti
 gation between the Philippines and Japan. Her work appears in journals in 
 anthropology\, history\, geography\, food studies\, and Asian studies. Her
  book\, Bananapocalypse: Plantation Capitalism and the Unmaking of Asia's 
 Banana Republic\, will be published by the University of California Press 
 in 2026. She is also co-editor of Halo-Halo Ecologies: The Emergent Enviro
 nments Behind Filipino Food (University of Hawaii Press 2025).Title  Air-c
 onditioned People and their Others: Class and Environmental Litigation in 
 the PhilippinesAbstract  In the Philippines\, naka-aircon or "air-conditio
 ned person" is a pejorative phrase describing someone as apathetic or deta
 ched from reality. It captures classed inequalities in access to hermetica
 lly sealed environments protected from the natural world. In Davao City\, 
 activists engaged in legal battles over chemical drift use the phrase to e
 xplain how the indifference of the elite dealt a fatal blow to their court
  case. This essay uses the idiomatic expression to argue that classed disp
 arities in the experience of the atmospheric uncommons bear repercussions 
 for the rule of law. While scholarship has considered how environmental di
 sparities are the result of state weakness\, this work considers how it ma
 y be the root of state inertia as well. Environmental equity\, then\, is n
 ot only a goal of legal equity\, but instead its prerequisite. The essay a
 lso invites reflection on the air-conditioned condition of field-based res
 earch in which the anthropologist\, too\, is uninnocent.About the Colloqui
 um SeriesEach Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers share and dis
 cuss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's res
 earch program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybersecurity\,
  and data management\, to the environment\, political economy\, and sustai
 nable food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world. Join us a
 t the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving tod
 ay. For more information\, see ourwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.indiana.
 edu/index.html). Meeting URL: https://iu.zoom.us/j/87671999280
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193737Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2328451-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-a
 lyssa-paredes
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image27715  lw
 _align_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/387/27715_paredes.rev.1755779713.w
 ebp 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><source type="image/png" srcset="https:/
 /events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/387/27715_paredes.rev.1
 755779713.png 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="387" 
 alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/
 image/gid/105/width/300/height/387/27715_paredes.rev.1755779713.png" data-
 max-w="471" data-max-h="607" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></pictu
 re></p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Alyssa Paredes is an anthropologist
  with interests in industrial agriculture\, transnational supply chains\, 
 and environmental litigation between the Philippines and Japan. Her work a
 ppears in journals in anthropology\, history\, geography\, food studies\, 
 and Asian studies. Her book\, <em>Bananapocalypse: Plantation Capitalism a
 nd the Unmaking of Asia's Banana Republic</em>\, will be published by the 
 University of California Press in 2026. She is also co-editor of <em>Halo-
 Halo Ecologies: The Emergent Environments Behind Filipino Food</em> (Unive
 rsity of Hawaii Press 2025).</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>  Air-cond
 itioned People and their Others: Class and Environmental Litigation in the
  Philippines</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  In the Philippines\, 
 <em>naka-aircon</em> or "air-conditioned person" is a pejorative phrase de
 scribing someone as apathetic or detached from reality. It captures classe
 d inequalities in access to hermetically sealed environments protected fro
 m the natural world. In Davao City\, activists engaged in legal battles ov
 er chemical drift use the phrase to explain how the indifference of the el
 ite dealt a fatal blow to their court case. This essay uses the idiomatic 
 expression to argue that classed disparities in the experience of the atmo
 spheric uncommons bear repercussions for the rule of law. While scholarshi
 p has considered how environmental disparities are the <em>result</em> of 
 state weakness\, this work considers how it may be the <em>root</em> of st
 ate inertia as well. Environmental equity\, then\, is not only a goal of l
 egal equity\, but instead its prerequisite. The essay also invites reflect
 ion on the air-conditioned condition of field-based research in which the 
 anthropologist\, too\, is uninnocent.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="n
 one">About the Colloquium Series</span></strong></p><p><span data-contrast
 ="none">Each Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers share and disc
 uss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's rese
 arch program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, 
 and data management\, to the environment\, political economy\, and sustain
 able food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world. Join us at
  the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving toda
 y. For more information\, see our</span><span data-contrast="none"><a clas
 s="external" href="https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="
 _blank" rel="noopener">website</a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</spa
 n></p> Meeting URL: https://iu.zoom.us/j/87671999280
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Karen Mack\, Ostrom Workshop Events Coordi
 nator\, workshop@iu.edu\n</p>
