X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2280622-21jpsi-speaker-series-digital-transformation-and-jap", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2280622-21jpsi-speaker-series-digital-transformation-and-jap"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2280622-21jpsi-speaker-series-digital-transformation-and-jap"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series: "Digital Transformation and Japan's Politic
 al Economy" (Prof. Kay Shimizu\, University of Pittsburgh)
DTSTART:20260922T200000Z
DTEND:20260922T211000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153410Z
UID:20260922T200000Z-2402233@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260922T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/162/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1750,2250/32373_20260922_21JPSI_SHIMIZU_FLYER_PNG.rev.178
 8482699.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, 
 and members of the community. Please join us!&#160\;  Professor Shimizu's 
 prepared remarks will be followed by a discussion/audience Q&amp\;A. GA/GI
 SB 1118About the talk:In this public lecture\, Prof. Shimizu will discuss 
 lessons for businesses and governments from Japan's experience with "digit
 al transformation." The digital transformation and demographic change are 
 usually seen as two separate but equally threatening events that foreshado
 w job replacement\, industrial decline and social bifurcation. Because Jap
 an is the world's frontrunner in demographic change with an ageing and shr
 inking society\, it is facing these two disruptions at the exact same time
 . This creates a "lucky moment"\, as it presents an opportunity to employ 
 one as a solution for the problems caused by the other. For example\, Japa
 n's traditional sectors are replaced by digital systems that demand fewer 
 people while offering new jobs. Emerging technologies are opening fresh op
 portunities for Japanese companies to compete globally. The twin disruptio
 ns are also upending Japan's political economy. As companies reinvent busi
 ness strategies and employees reskill to pursue individual careers\, the s
 tate is reorganizing to find a new role in balancing the unfolding demands
  of the digital economy.This event is co-sponsored by the Cybersecurity an
 d Global Policy Program and Department of Political Science.About the spea
 ker:Kay Shimizuis a Research Professor in the Graduate School of Internati
 onal and Public Affairs at the University of Pittsburgh. She earned her B.
 A. in Economics and International Relations and her Ph.D. in Political Sci
 ence from Stanford University. Her research addresses institutional design
  and their effects on economic governance with a focus on East Asia. She i
 s the author of Betting on the Farm: Institutional Change in Japanese Agri
 culture (with Patricia Maclachlan)\, and The Digital Transformation and Ja
 pan's Political Economy (with Ulrike Schaede). Her current research is abo
 ut the confluence of demographic change and the digital transformation in 
 the East Asian context.This event is made possible thanks to generous fina
 ncial support from the Japan Foundation. All 21JPSI events are 100% in-per
 son unless otherwise noted.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260904T004514Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2280622-21jpsi-speaker-series-digita
 l-transformation-and-jap
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This public event is open to all student
 s\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community. Please join us!&#160\;
 </p><p>  Professor Shimizu's prepared remarks will be followed by a discus
 sion/audience Q&amp\;A. <strong>GA/GISB 1118</strong></p><p><span style="t
 ext-decoration: underline\;"><strong>About the talk</strong>:</span></p><p
 ><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feat
 ure-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none">In this public 
 lecture\, Prof. Shimizu will discuss lessons for businesses and government
 s from Japan's experience with "digital transformation."</span> The digita
 l transformation and demographic change are usually seen as two separate b
 ut equally threatening events that foreshadow job replacement\, industrial
  decline and social bifurcation. Because Japan is the world's frontrunner 
 in demographic change with an ageing and shrinking society\, it is facing 
 these two disruptions at the exact same time. This creates a "lucky moment
 "\, as it presents an opportunity to employ one as a solution for the prob
 lems caused by the other. For example\, Japan's traditional sectors are re
 placed by digital systems that demand fewer people while offering new jobs
 . Emerging technologies are opening fresh opportunities for Japanese compa
 nies to compete globally. The twin disruptions are also upending Japan's p
 olitical economy. As companies reinvent business strategies and employees 
 reskill to pursue individual careers\, the state is reorganizing to find a
  new role in balancing the unfolding demands of the digital economy.</p><p
 ><em>This event is co-sponsored by the Cybersecurity and Global Policy Pro
 gram and Department of Political Science.</em></p><div><strong style="font
 -size: 16px\;"><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">About the speake
 r:</span></strong></div><div class="c-vertical-tabs" data-ms-component="ve
 rtical-tabs" data-vertical-tabs-display="vertical"><div class="c-vertical-
 tabs__item" data-vertical-tabs-component="tab" data-vertical-tabs-state="i
 s-open"><div class="c-vertical-tabs__content vertical-tabs__content-long o
 pen" data-vertical-tabs-component="content" aria-hidden="false" id="collap
 sible-1" role="region"><div class="s-wysiwyg"><div class="s-wysiwyg"><stro
 ng style="font-size: 16px\;">Kay Shimizu</strong><span style="font-size: 1
 6px\;">is a Research Professor in the Graduate School of International and
  Public Affairs at the University of Pittsburgh. She earned her B.A. in Ec
 onomics and International Relations and her Ph.D. in Political Science fro
 m Stanford University. Her research addresses institutional design and the
 ir effects on economic governance with a focus on East Asia. She is the au
 thor of Betting on the Farm: Institutional Change in Japanese Agriculture 
 (with Patricia Maclachlan)\, and The Digital Transformation and Japan's Po
 litical Economy (with Ulrike Schaede). Her current research is about the c
 onfluence of demographic change and the digital transformation in the East
  Asian context.</span></div><div class="s-wysiwyg"></div><div class="s-wys
 iwyg"><span style="font-size: 16px\;"><em>This event is made possible than
 ks to generous financial support from the Japan Foundation. All 21JPSI eve
 nts are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted.</em></span></div></div></di
 v></div></div>
