 r">Register here</a>)</p><p style="text-align: left\;">  &#160\;</p><p>  E
 very night for the first three months of 1937\, 23-year-old Albert Quint c
 ompleted a page of his diary in the house he shared with his working-class
  parents in rural Wiltshire (England).</p><p>  He described how his shopwo
 rk sustained his obsession with cinema and funded the acting classes which
  he saw as a stepping stone to a career on the stage. Alongside\, he allud
 ed to his 'bad habits' and his erotic interest in the soldiers stationed a
 t nearby military bases.</p><p>  Albert's testimony is the cue for this ta
 lk\, which explores how work – most especially in the service sector\, t
 heatre\, and armed forces - was practically\, aspirationally\, and erotica
 lly significant to queer everyday lives in inter and post war Britain. Tra
 cing Albert's life back and forward from his tantalising diary entries shi
 nes light on the literal labour involved in 'doing being ordinary' (Sacks\
 , 1984) and\, I argue\, in 'doing being deviant' (Love\, 2015).</p><p><str
 ong>Matt Cook</strong> is the inaugural Jonathan Cooper Professor of the H
