X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2391956-lecture-polishsouth-netherlandish-humanist"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Lecture: "Polish–South Netherlandish Humanist Networks during th
 e 16th and 17th Centuries" with Michał Czerenkiewicz
DTSTART:20260925T180000Z
DTEND:20260925T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153744Z
UID:20260925T180000Z-2402262@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260925T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/181/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2505,3200/32150_Micha_Czerenkiewicz.rev.1787580201.jpg
DESCRIPTION:The mutual humanistic connections between the Polish-Lithuania
 n Commonwealth and the Southern Low Countries embraced\, among others\, th
 e academic life and literary production. The networks formed by the humani
 sts and the students who traveled to the University of Leuven are a clear 
 example of the vivid role of the Latin literary culture.  Michał Czerenki
 ewicz is an assistant professor at the Faculty of Polish Studies at the Ja
 giellonian University in Kraków. His scientific work focuses on early mod
 ern book history\, Neo-Latin literature\, and Polish-South Netherlandish c
 onnections\, mainly in the 17th century. He authored three books and is an
  editorial board member of the "Terminus" journal.  Cosponsored by the Pol
 ish Studies Center\, The Byrnes Insitutes (REEI)\, and the Institute for E
 uropean Studies\,&#160\;
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193744Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2391956-lecture-polishsouth-netherla
 ndish-humanist
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The mutual humanistic connections betwee
 n the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Southern Low Countries embrac
 ed\, among others\, the academic life and literary production. The network
 s formed by the humanists and the students who traveled to the University 
 of Leuven are a clear example of the vivid role of the Latin literary cult
 ure.</p><p>  Michał Czerenkiewicz is an assistant professor at the Facult
 y of Polish Studies at the Jagiellonian University in Kraków. His scienti
 fic work focuses on early modern book history\, Neo-Latin literature\, and
  Polish-South Netherlandish connections\, mainly in the 17th century. He a
 uthored three books and is an editorial board member of the "Terminus" jou
 rnal.</p><p>  Cosponsored by the Polish Studies Center\, The Byrnes Insitu
 tes (REEI)\, and the Institute for European Studies\,&#160\;</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2402262
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/181/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,2505\,3200/32150_Micha_Czerenkiewicz.rev.178
 7580201.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Michał Czerenkiewicz
