BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2295095-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-vaishnavi-tripuraneni", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2295095-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-vaishnavi-tripuraneni"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2295095-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-vaishnavi-tripuraneni"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Vaishnavi Tripuraneni
DTSTART:20260928T160000Z
DTEND:20260928T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153740Z
UID:20260928T160000Z-2402259@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260928T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,640,426/12533_globe-1130870_640.rev.1659031295.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her) is an Assistant Professor
  in the department of Geography and Spatial Sciences at the University of 
 Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist whose work draws on frameworks
  from agrarian political economy\, human geography\, feminist approaches t
 o people-environment interactions\, and human-dimensions of environmental 
 change. Using mixed methods\, she has conducted extensive research in sout
 h India on agrarian debt and biodiversity conservation in human-managed la
 ndscapes\, human dimensions of invasive plant management in California\, a
 nd climate resilience in Delaware.  Prior to joining UD\, she was a postdo
 ctoral scholar in the Human Dimensions Lab\, Purdue University. She has a 
 Ph.D. in Environmental Studies from the University of Wisconsin-Madison. A
 t UD\, Tripuraneni serves as Director of the Environmental Studies undergr
 aduate program. She teaches human geography and environmental studies cour
 ses and applies community-engaged teaching and learning pedagogy to provid
 e students real-world experience while addressing local\, place-based\, is
 sues identified by the community.Title  Life and Debt: production and repr
 oduction in agrarian south IndiaAbstract  Debt\, a fundamental part of agr
 arian life and a key contributor to agrarian change\, undermines farmer au
 tonomy\, limits future farm investments\, and sets the emotional and econo
 mic conditions of households. Farmers juggle debts to meet not just agricu
 ltural needs but also everyday household reproductive expenses\, from medi
 cal bills to marriage expenses\, in ways not well understood. This researc
 h explores the relationship between productive and reproductive debt in ru
 ral Telangana\, India. Life and debt (oral) histories and surveys of farme
 rs capture experiences of indebtedness and struggles to repay loans. The a
 nalysis shows that: 1) reproductive debt is a significant part of overall 
 debt\; 2) productive and reproductive debt are intertwined in complicated 
 ways\; and 3) these entanglements lead to cascading patterns of debt\, inc
 luding borrowing to repay previous loans. These together point to the fina
 ncialization of reproduction in the agrarian household\, with implications
  for lending\, social service provision\, and smallholder survival.About t
 he Colloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers sh
 are and discuss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Wor
 kshop's research program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybe
 rsecurity\, and data management\, to the environment\, political economy\,
  and sustainable food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world
 . Join us at the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is e
 volving today. For more information\, see ourwebsite (https://ostromworksh
 op.indiana.edu/index.html). Meeting URL: https://iu.zoom.us/j/87111484380
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T194849Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2295095-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-v
 aishnavi-tripuraneni
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong><span data-contrast="none"><pictur
 e class="lw_image lw_image31395  lw_align_left lw_block"><source type="ima
 ge/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height
 /300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.rev.1782501567.webp 1x" data-origin="
 responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/
 image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.rev.178
 2501567.jpg 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="300" al
 t="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/im
 age/gid/105/width/300/height/300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.rev.17825
 01567.jpg" data-max-w="590" data-max-h="590" loading="lazy" data-optimized
 ="true"/></picture></span></strong></p><p><strong><span data-contrast="non
 e">Bio</span></strong></p><p>  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her) is an Assis
 tant Professor in the department of Geography and Spatial Sciences at the 
 University of Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist whose work draws
  on frameworks from agrarian political economy\, human geography\, feminis
 t approaches to people-environment interactions\, and human-dimensions of 
 environmental change. Using mixed methods\, she has conducted extensive re
 search in south India on agrarian debt and biodiversity conservation in hu
 man-managed landscapes\, human dimensions of invasive plant management in 
 California\, and climate resilience in Delaware.</p><p>  Prior to joining 
 UD\, she was a postdoctoral scholar in the Human Dimensions Lab\, Purdue U
 niversity. She has a Ph.D. in Environmental Studies from the University of
  Wisconsin-Madison. At UD\, Tripuraneni serves as Director of the Environm
 ental Studies undergraduate program. She teaches human geography and envir
 onmental studies courses and applies community-engaged teaching and learni
 ng pedagogy to provide students real-world experience while addressing loc
 al\, place-based\, issues identified by the community.</p><p><strong><span
  data-contrast="none">Title</span></strong></p><p>  Life and Debt: product
 ion and reproduction in agrarian south India</p><p><strong>Abstract</stron
 g></p><p>  Debt\, a fundamental part of agrarian life and a key contributo
 r to agrarian change\, undermines farmer autonomy\, limits future farm inv
 estments\, and sets the emotional and economic conditions of households. F
 armers juggle debts to meet not just agricultural needs but also everyday 
 household reproductive expenses\, from medical bills to marriage expenses\
 , in ways not well understood. This research explores the relationship bet
 ween productive and reproductive debt in rural Telangana\, India. Life and
  debt (oral) histories and surveys of farmers capture experiences of indeb
 tedness and struggles to repay loans. The analysis shows that: 1) reproduc
 tive debt is a significant part of overall debt\; 2) productive and reprod
 uctive debt are intertwined in complicated ways\; and 3) these entanglemen
 ts lead to cascading patterns of debt\, including borrowing to repay previ
 ous loans. These together point to the financialization of reproduction in
  the agrarian household\, with implications for lending\, social service p
 rovision\, and smallholder survival.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="no
 ne">About the Colloquium Series</span></strong></p><p><span data-contrast=
 "none">Each Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers share and discu
 ss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's resea
 rch program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, a
 nd data management\, to the environment\, political economy\, and sustaina
 ble food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world. Join us at 
 the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today
 . For more information\, see our</span><span data-contrast="none"><a class
 ="external" href="https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_
 blank" rel="noopener">website</a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span
 ></p> Meeting URL: https://iu.zoom.us/j/87111484380
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Karen Mack\, Ostrom Workshop Events Coordi
 nator\, workshop@iu.edu\n</p>
