SUMMARY:John Sanders | J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture
DTSTART:20260929T223000Z
DTEND:20260930T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20260819T124300Z
UID:20260929T223000Z-2386397@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260929T223000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,738,922/32080_john-sanders-headshot-by-BruceCole-2023-WEB.
 rev.1787157375.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:In this lecture\,"Seeds of Regionalism—The Clauss Legacy: Ea
 rly Modernism in the South\," John Lynch  Sanders\, FAIA\, explores 
 an early chapter in the architectural legacy of Alfred and Jane West Claus
 s\, creators of America's first modern deed-restricted subdivision located
  in Knoxville\, Tennessee's "Little Switzerland". Delivered from a first-h
 and perspective\, Sanders details a 13-year immersive journey of conservin
 g and restoring these historically neglected structures. The presentation 
 goes beyond traditional archival history to examine the performative\, phy
 sical processes of preservation. By analyzing the tactical interventions r
 equired to uncover the original designs\, the lecture demonstrates how the
  Clausses successfully synthesized international modernism with regional A
 ppalachian topography and vernacular materials.Sanders is a founding par
 tner of Sanders Pace Architects\, established in 2002 to focus on desi
 gn opportunities within Knoxville's urban core and its surrounding neighbo
 rhoods. His passion for design is reflected in the firm's most challenging
  projects\, which prioritize the thoughtful rehabilitation of neglected bu
 ildings and the elevation of overlooked project typologies. Beyond his pra
 ctice\, Sanders is deeply committed to advancing the profession through 
 teaching\, mentorship\, and civic engagement.An active member of the Ameri
 can Institute of Architects (AIA)\, Sanders has served as President of t
 he AIA East Tennessee chapter and on the AIA Tennessee State Board. Throug
 hout his board tenures\, he was instrumental in creating a signature licen
 see program that formally recognizes and celebrates the milestones of emer
 ging professionals at local and state levels.Sanders is a graduate of the 
 University of Tennessee\, where he has also shared his expertise as an Adj
 unct Lecturer. In recognition of his contributions to the discipline\, he 
 was elevated to the prestigious AIA College of Fellows in 2018 and in 2024
  was awarded the AIA East Tennessee Gold Medal\, the chapter's highest hon
 or\, along with his business partner Brandon Pace\, FAIA.The J. Irwin Mill
 er Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent wor
 king artists and architects to The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street i
 n Columbus\, Indiana.
