BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 234": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2406254-burke-lecture-series-dr-huey-copeland"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_234
SUMMARY:Burke Lecture Series: Dr. Huey Copeland
DTSTART:20261006T200000Z
DTEND:20261006T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260903T131704Z
UID:20261006T200000Z-2406351@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261006T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,860,1146/32346_Copeland_Book_Cover.rev.1788441606.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:In this lecture drawn from his new book project\, Huey Copelan
 d considers how disciplinary structures differently value and tend towards
  the racialized historicity of the aesthetic\, above all to those blackene
 d beings whose everywhere present yet suppressed exertions continue to pro
 vide the preconditions for the emergence of the visual\, the modern world\
 , and the work of artists on the engendering of both.  Dr. Huey Copeland i
 s the Andrew W. Mellon Chair and Professor of Modern Art and Black Study a
 t the Universtiy of Pittsburgh. Copeland's work interrogates African/Diasp
 oric\, American and European artistic praxis from the late 18th-century to
  the present with an emphasis on articulations of blackness in the Western
  visual field. In his interdisciplinary research\, Copeland focuses on the
  intersections of race and gender\, subject and object\, the aesthetic and
  its others from a black feminist perspective that reveals the biases and 
 elisions of the discipline. Rather than assume the redemptive power of art
 \, he aims to push history against the grain in exploring the constitutive
  relationship between the capture of black life and the production of cult
 ural property in the modern transatlantic world.  &#160\;  Lecture will be
  held until 5 pm followed by questions. Reception to follow.Sponsored by t
 he Robert E. &amp\; Avis Tarrant Burke Lecture Fund.
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
LAST-MODIFIED:20260903T171704Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2406254-burke-lecture-series-dr-huey
 -copeland
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  In this lecture drawn from his new book 
 project\, Huey Copeland considers how disciplinary structures differently 
 value and tend towards the racialized historicity of the aesthetic\, above
  all to those blackened beings whose everywhere present yet suppressed exe
 rtions continue to provide the preconditions for the emergence of the visu
 al\, the modern world\, and the work of artists on the engendering of both
 .</p><p>  Dr. Huey Copeland is the Andrew W. Mellon Chair and Professor of
  Modern Art and Black Study at the Universtiy of Pittsburgh. Copeland's wo
 rk interrogates African/Diasporic\, American and European artistic praxis 
 from the late 18th-century to the present with an emphasis on articulation
 s of blackness in the Western visual field. In his interdisciplinary resea
 rch\, Copeland focuses on the intersections of race and gender\, subject a
 nd object\, the aesthetic and its others from a black feminist perspective
  that reveals the biases and elisions of the discipline. Rather than assum
 e the redemptive power of art\, he aims to push history against the grain 
 in exploring the constitutive relationship between the capture of black li
 fe and the production of cultural property in the modern transatlantic wor
 ld.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p>  Lecture will be held until 5 pm followed by qu
 estions. Reception to follow.</p><p><em>Sponsored by the Robert E. &amp\; 
 Avis Tarrant Burke Lecture Fund.</em></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Meg Calvert<br />\n  <a href="mailto:mcalv
 er@iu.edu">mcalver@iu.edu</a>&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2406351
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/91/width/168/height
 /168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,860\,1146/32346_Copeland_Book_Cover.rev.17884
 41606.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:"Touched by the Mother: Black Men\, American Art
 \, Feminist Horizons" by Dr. Huey Copeland
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Dr. Huey Copeland\, Andrew W. Mellon Chair &amp
 \; Professor of Modern Art and Black Study\, presents: <em>All the Things 
 Art History</em> <em>Could Be by Now if Blackness Were its Mother: Reflect
 ions on Discipline and Value</em>\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:Eskenazi Museum of Art
END:VEVENT
