SUMMARY:Korean Rice Cake Workshop
DTSTART:20261008T150000Z
DTEND:20261008T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153505Z
UID:20261008T150000Z-2402248@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261008T150000Z
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, October 8thAvailable sessions:&#160\;  Session 1: 1
 1:00 - 12:30 p.m.  Session 2: 2:00 - 3:30 p.m.At GISB 1060&#160\;355 N Eag
 leson Av.  Bloomington 47405  &#160\;  This event is free and open to the 
 public.&#160\;  All materials will be provided.&#160\;  RSVP ONLY  &#160\;
 About Songpyeon:  Songpyeon (송편) is a traditional Korean treat made of
  glutinous rice. It is a type of tteok (떡)\, small rice cakes\, traditio
 nally eaten during the Korean autumn harvest festival\, Chuseok (석). Son
 gpyeon symbolizes a full moon and was used for ancestral celebrations alon
 g with other foods to show appreciation for the year's harvest. Koreans ho
 ped and prayed to help them avoid misfortune.About&#160\;Jennifer Lee Ban:
 Jennifer Lee Ban\, also known as Mama Ban\, is the founder of Rice Blossom
 s\, a modern Korean dessert brand rooted in tradition and wellness. After 
 becoming a new mom\, she struggled to find wholesome snacks for her daught
 ers while seeking a creative outlet after stepping away from her career as
  a professional interior designer. Her search led her to the art of Korean
  dessert-making\, where she discovered beautifully crafted treats that wer
 e both nourishing and deeply meaningful. Mama Ban was drawn to traditional
  Korean rice cakes for their natural qualities—they are gluten-free\, of
 ten vegan\, and thoughtfully made. Today\, through Rice Blossoms\, she rei
 magines these time-honored desserts with a modern sensibility\, steaming e
 ach one with care and intention.&#160\;  You will be able to take your Son
 gpyeon home!  Light refreshments will be served.&#160\;
GEO:39.170011;-86.517418
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193505Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2376428-korean-rice-cake-workshop
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Thursday\, October 8th<br /></stro
 ng>Available sessions:&#160\;<br />  Session 1: 11:00 - 12:30 p.m.<br />  
 Session 2: 2:00 - 3:30 p.m.</p><p><strong>At GISB 1060&#160\;<br /></stron
 g>355 N Eagleson Av.<br />  Bloomington 47405</p><p style="text-align: cen
 ter\;">  &#160\;</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  This event is free a
 nd open to the public.&#160\;<br />  All materials will be provided.&#160\
 ;<strong><br /><br />  RSVP ONLY</strong></p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>Ab
 out Songpyeon:</strong><br />  Songpyeon (송편) is a traditional Korean 
 treat made of glutinous rice. It is a type of tteok (떡)\, small rice cak
 es\, traditionally eaten during the Korean autumn harvest festival\, Chuse
 ok (추석). Songpyeon symbolizes a full moon and was used for ancestral c
 elebrations along with other foods to show appreciation for the year's har
 vest. Koreans hoped and prayed to help them avoid misfortune.</p><p><stron
 g>About&#160\;</strong><strong>Jennifer Lee Ban:<br /></strong>Jennifer Le
 e Ban\, also known as Mama Ban\, is the founder of Rice Blossoms\, a moder
 n Korean dessert brand rooted in tradition and wellness. After becoming a 
 new mom\, she struggled to find wholesome snacks for her daughters while s
 eeking a creative outlet after stepping away from her career as a professi
 onal interior designer. Her search led her to the art of Korean dessert-ma
 king\, where she discovered beautifully crafted treats that were both nour
 ishing and deeply meaningful. Mama Ban was drawn to traditional Korean ric
 e cakes for their natural qualities—they are gluten-free\, often vegan\,
  and thoughtfully made. Today\, through Rice Blossoms\, she reimagines the
 se time-honored desserts with a modern sensibility\, steaming each one wit
 h care and intention.&#160\;</p><p style="text-align: center\;"><br />  Yo
 u will be able to take your Songpyeon home!<br />  Light refreshments will
  be served.&#160\;</p>
