BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 11": "https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2385188-story-sajik-kim-exhibition"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:Story: Sajik Kim exhibition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261009
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261010
DTSTAMP:20260818T163935Z
UID:20261009T040000Z-2385188@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261009
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,798,977/32058_Sajik-Kim-The_Two-Headed_White_Serpent-inkje
 t-print-2020-WEB.rev.1787085243.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Lectures, talks, seminars, and confe
 rences
DESCRIPTION:Artist Talk: Friday\, September 11\, 12–1 pm\, Shreve Audito
 rium-Global and International Studies Building (355 N. Eagleson Avenue)Ope
 ning reception: Friday\, September 11\, 6–8 pm\, Grunwald GalleryArtist 
 Performance: Friday\, September 11\, 7 pm\, Grunwald GalleryCo-curated by 
 Osamu James Nakagawa (Ruth N. Halls Distinguished Professor of Photography
 ) and Linda Tien (Director of the Grunwald Gallery)\, photography exhibiti
 on STORY is a creation myth drawn from the lived experience of Zainichi&#1
 60\;artist Sajik Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a third-generation Kore
 an national. Infused with theatrical elements and embodied performance\, S
 TORY explores how one might establish a sense of self and live with rooted
 ness amid the constant flux of identity.Directed by Kim herself\,STORYpose
 s the question: What is homeland? Every nation and people possess a creati
 on myth that narrates the beginning of their community and sustains their 
 collective identity. Yet\, forZainichi artistKim\, born and raised in Japa
 n as a third-generation Korean national\, there was a persistent sense of 
 not being able to fully embody a fixed ethnic identity. From this position
 \, Kim attempts to weave a personal "creation myth" drawn from her lived e
 xperience.In developing this series\, Kim conducted research within Japan\
 , incorporating elements from everyday life—such as food\, clothing\, ho
 using\, education\, and festivals as well as encounters with people and hi
 storical events.STORYthus explores how one might establish a sense of self
  and live with rootedness amid the constant flux of identity. Furthermore\
 , it seeks to resonate with and offer insight to those who\, in an increas
 ingly complex world\, must navigate similarly unstable identities.Employin
 g the methodology of staged photography\, theSTORYseries is orchestrated t
 hrough comprehensive direction—including lighting\, makeup\, costume\, a
 nd posing. While photographic in form\, the works are simultaneously infus
 ed with theatrical elements and embodied performance.
GEO:39.169111;-86.518919
LAST-MODIFIED:20260826T195417Z
LOCATION:FINE ARTS BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2385188-story-sajik-kim-exhibitio
 n
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-contrast="none"><strong>Artist 
 Talk:</strong> Friday\, September 11\, 12–1 pm\, Shreve Auditorium-Globa
 l and International Studies Building (355 N. Eagleson Avenue)</span></p><p
 ><span data-contrast="none"><strong>Opening reception</strong>: Friday\, S
 eptember 11\, 6–8 pm\, Grunwald Gallery</span></p><p><span data-contrast
 ="none"><strong>Artist Performance:</strong> Friday\, September 11\, 7 pm\
 , Grunwald Gallery</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\
 ,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:276}"></span></p><p><span class="NormalTextRun SC
 XW175992698 BCX8">Co-curated by <span style="caret-color: #000000\; color:
  #000000\; font-family: Arial\, Helvetica\, sans-serif\; font-size: 16px\;
  font-style: normal\; font-variant-caps: normal\; font-weight: 400\; lette
 r-spacing: normal\; orphans: 2\; text-align: left\; text-indent: 0px\; tex
 t-transform: none\; white-space: normal\; widows: 2\; word-spacing: 0px\; 
 -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px\; background-color: #ffffff\; text-decorati
 on: none\; display: inline !important\; float: none\;">Osamu James Nakagaw
 a</span> (Ruth N. Halls Distinguished Professor of Photography) and Linda 
 Tien (Director of the Grunwald Gallery)\, photography exhibition STORY is 
 a creation myth drawn from the lived experience of <span style="caret-colo
 r: #000000\; color: #000000\; font-family: Arial\, Helvetica\, sans-serif\
 ; font-size: 16px\; font-style: normal\; font-variant-caps: normal\; font-
 weight: 400\; letter-spacing: normal\; orphans: 2\; text-align: left\; tex
 t-indent: 0px\; text-transform: none\; white-space: normal\; widows: 2\; w
 ord-spacing: 0px\; -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0px\; background-color: #fff
 fff\; text-decoration: none\; display: inline !important\; float: none\;">
 Zainichi</span>&#160\;artist Sajik Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a thi
 rd-generation Korean national. Infused with theatrical elements and embodi
 ed performance\, STORY explores how one might establish a sense of self an
 d live with rootedness amid the constant flux of identity.</span></p><p><s
 pan data-contrast="auto">Directed by Kim herself\,</span><i><span data-con
 trast="auto">STORY</span></i><span data-contrast="auto">poses the question
 : What is homeland? Every nation and people possess a creation myth that n
 arrates the beginning of their community and sustains their collective ide
 ntity. Yet\, for</span><span data-contrast="none">Zainichi artist</span><s
 pan data-contrast="auto">Kim\, born and raised in Japan as a third-generat
 ion Korean national\, there was a persistent sense of not being able to fu
 lly embody a fixed ethnic identity. From this position\, Kim attempts to w
 eave a personal "creation myth" drawn from her lived experience.</span><sp
 an data-ccp-props="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">In develop
 ing this series\, Kim conducted research within Japan\, incorporating elem
 ents from everyday life—such as food\, clothing\, housing\, education\, 
 and festivals as well as encounters with people and historical events.</sp
 an><i><span data-contrast="auto">STORY</span></i><span data-contrast="auto
 ">thus explores how one might establish a sense of self and live with root
 edness amid the constant flux of identity. Furthermore\, it seeks to reson
 ate with and offer insight to those who\, in an increasingly complex world
 \, must navigate similarly unstable identities.</span><span data-ccp-props
 ="{}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">Employing the methodology o
 f staged photography\, the</span><i><span data-contrast="auto">STORY</span
 ></i><span data-contrast="auto">series is orchestrated through comprehensi
 ve direction—including lighting\, makeup\, costume\, and posing. While p
 hotographic in form\, the works are simultaneously infused with theatrical
  elements and embodied performance.</span><span data-ccp-props="{}"></span
 ></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ALL-DAY:1
