 "><span class="NormalTextRun SCXW54583245 BCX0">Siddiqui's work—manifest
 ed as objects\, images\, texts\, and events—links design experimentation
  to public engagement.</span></span> He is interested in the role that int
 eriors play in architectural experiments while advocating for experimentat
 ion in interiors as a distinct field of knowledge.</span><span data-ccp-pr
 ops="{&quot\;335559685&quot\;:0}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast="none"
 >Siddiqui's book</span><i><span data-contrast="none">Oblique Experiments: 
 Claude Parent&#8217\;s Architectural Installations (1969–1975)</span></i
 ><span data-contrast="none">is one of the first English-language monograph
 s on French avant-garde architect Claude Parent and the first comprehensiv
 e study of his experimental interior installations from the 1970s. He rece
 ntly edited</span><i><span data-contrast="none">Interior Experiments</span
 ></i><span data-contrast="none">\, a special triple-issue of</span><i><spa
