BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388122-byrnes-institute-colloquium-madeline-stull"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Byrnes Institute Colloquium: Madeline Stull\, "Makeshift Modernity
 : The (In)Formal Urbanization of Interwar Belgrade"
DTSTART:20261016T180000Z
DTEND:20261016T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153748Z
UID:20261016T180000Z-2402264@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261016T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/182/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,400,400/32111_1620338599768.rev.1787245334.jpg
DESCRIPTION:This talk examines the (in)formal dynamics of Belgrade's urban
 ization during the interwar period\, focusing on the parallel development 
 of residential and municipal infrastructure in two adjacent neighborhoods:
  Jatagan-mala\, the city's largest informal settlement\, and Kotež Neimar
 \, a privately developed neighborhood. Challenging narratives that margina
 lize the urban poor as peripheral to the city's development\, Stull argues
  that informal settlement and formal planning were mutually constitutive p
 rocesses. Drawing on archival documents\, urban plans\, and contemporary r
 eporting\, she shows how a regulatory and property-centered modernizing lo
 gic structured Belgrade's growth through displacement and dispossession. M
 unicipal anxieties about the city's form and order materialized in the str
 eets\, producing hierarchies of urban belonging and practices of ownership
  grounded in bourgeois respectability\, with violent consequences for the 
 city's majority-poor population.  Madeline Stull is a Ph.D. candidate in E
 ast European History (with minors in Cultural History and Digital Humaniti
 es) at Indiana University\, Bloomington. She earned a Master's and Bachelo
 r of Arts in European History from Arizona State University and Barrett\, 
 the Honor's College in 2020 and 2021 respectively. Her research has been s
 upported by the U.S. Fulbright Commission and an American Councils researc
 h fellowship. Madeline's most recent publication\, "The Fantasy of Reason:
  Reevaluating Belgrade's 20th Century Urban Plans through Borislav Pekić'
 s Houses\," is available in Historical Geography issue no. 52. She is curr
 ently working on a dissertation entitled\, Makeshift Modernity: The Creati
 on of Belgrade at the Intersection of (In)Formality\, which historizes and
  spatializes the marginalization of Belgrade's urban Others to better unde
 rstand the genealogy of modern urban management practices in a newly forme
 d\, peripheral nation.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193748Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388122-byrnes-institute-colloquium-
 madeline-stull
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This talk examines the (in)formal dynami
 cs of Belgrade's urbanization during the interwar period\, focusing on the
  parallel development of residential and municipal infrastructure in two a
 djacent neighborhoods: Jatagan-mala\, the city's largest informal settleme
 nt\, and Kotež Neimar\, a privately developed neighborhood. Challenging n
 arratives that marginalize the urban poor as peripheral to the city's deve
 lopment\, Stull argues that informal settlement and formal planning were m
 utually constitutive processes. Drawing on archival documents\, urban plan
 s\, and contemporary reporting\, she shows how a regulatory and property-c
 entered modernizing logic structured Belgrade's growth through displacemen
 t and dispossession. Municipal anxieties about the city's form and order m
 aterialized in the streets\, producing hierarchies of urban belonging and 
 practices of ownership grounded in bourgeois respectability\, with violent
  consequences for the city's majority-poor population.</p><p>  Madeline St
 ull is a Ph.D. candidate in East European History (with minors in Cultural
  History and Digital Humanities) at Indiana University\, Bloomington. She 
 earned a Master's and Bachelor of Arts in European History from Arizona St
 ate University and Barrett\, the Honor's College in 2020 and 2021 respecti
 vely. Her research has been supported by the U.S. Fulbright Commission and
  an American Councils research fellowship. Madeline's most recent publicat
 ion\, "The Fantasy of Reason: Reevaluating Belgrade's 20th Century Urban P
 lans through Borislav Pekić's Houses\," is available in Historical Geogra
 phy issue no. 52. She is currently working on a dissertation entitled\, Ma
 keshift Modernity: The Creation of Belgrade at the Intersection of (In)For
 mality\, which historizes and spatializes the marginalization of Belgrade'
 s urban Others to better understand the genealogy of modern urban manageme
 nt practices in a newly formed\, peripheral nation.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2402264
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/182/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,400\,400/32111_1620338599768.rev.1787245334.
 jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Madeline Stull
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
END:VEVENT
