BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2381038-lecture-the-tragedy-of-post-soviet-science-russia-an"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Lecture: "The Tragedy of Post-Soviet Science: Russia and Ukraine\,
  1986–2022" with Michael D. Gordin
DTSTART:20261020T190000Z
DTEND:20261020T200000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153749Z
UID:20261020T190000Z-2402265@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261020T190000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/182/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1600,1200/31949_IMG_2292.rev.1786631617.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:At its height in the late 1980s\, the Soviet science system wa
 s the largest infrastructure of science and technology of any country in t
 he world. After the dissolution of the Soviet Union in late 1991\, all fif
 teen successor states not only had to contract their science systems\, but
  also to create national boundaries between enterprises and communities th
 at had once been shared. This presentation traces the course of the fragme
 ntation of Soviet science in the two states that inherited the bulk of the
  science complex\, Russia and Ukraine\, from the 1986 nuclear disaster in 
 Chornobyl to the full-scale Russian invasion of Ukraine in February 2022\,
  with particular emphasis on the space program\, nuclear weapons\, and the
  relationship of each state to the internationalized scientific community.
   Michael Gordin specializes in the history of the modern physical science
 s and Russian\, European\, and American history\; as of July 2024\, he ser
 ves as Dean of the College at Princeton University. Professor Gordin teach
 es lecture courses in the history of modern science from 1700 to 1970 (His
 tory 292) and the history of contemporary science from 1970 to the present
 \, and is currently working on a history of the impact of the collapse of 
 the Soviet Union on science inside and outside of Russia and Ukraine.Cospo
 nsored by The Byrnes Institute (REEI)\, the Department of History\, the De
 partment of History and Philosophy of Science and Medicine\, and the Steph
 en F. Cohen Chair of Russian History.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193749Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2381038-lecture-the-tragedy-of-post-
 soviet-science-russia-an
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  At its height in the late 1980s\, the So
 viet science system was the largest infrastructure of science and technolo
 gy of any country in the world. After the dissolution of the Soviet Union 
 in late 1991\, all fifteen successor states not only had to contract their
  science systems\, but also to create national boundaries between enterpri
 ses and communities that had once been shared. This presentation traces th
 e course of the fragmentation of Soviet science in the two states that inh
 erited the bulk of the science complex\, Russia and Ukraine\, from the 198
 6 nuclear disaster in Chornobyl to the full-scale Russian invasion of Ukra
 ine in February 2022\, with particular emphasis on the space program\, nuc
 lear weapons\, and the relationship of each state to the internationalized
  scientific community.</p><p>  Michael Gordin specializes in the history o
 f the modern physical sciences and Russian\, European\, and American histo
 ry\; as of July 2024\, he serves as Dean of the College at Princeton Unive
 rsity. Professor Gordin teaches lecture courses in the history of modern s
 cience from 1700 to 1970 (History 292) and the history of contemporary sci
 ence from 1970 to the present\, and is currently working on a history of t
 he impact of the collapse of the Soviet Union on science inside and outsid
 e of Russia and Ukraine.</p><p><em>Cosponsored by The Byrnes Institute (RE
 EI)\, the Department of History\, the Department of History and Philosophy
  of Science and Medicine\, and the Stephen F. Cohen Chair of Russian Histo
 ry.</em></p>
