X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Frontlines & Futures: The United Nations General Assembly Debrief
 —Power\, Rhetoric\, and the Reality of Global Governance
DTSTART:20261022T160000Z
DTEND:20261022T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260601T125644Z
UID:20261022T160000Z-2314767@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261022T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/135/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1024,1536/28558_Indiana_University_Foreign_Policy_Event.r
 ev.1759952589.png
CATEGORIES:Career development,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,T
 raining, non-credit classes, and learning opportunities
DESCRIPTION:Overview: Every September\, world leaders assemble in New York
  for the United Nations General Assembly to outline strategic priorities a
 nd negotiate multilateral commitments. Yet when the high-level statements 
 wrap up\, international institutions face the hard work of implementation 
 in a system where compliance is largely voluntary.  In this edition of Fro
 ntlines &amp\; Futures\, Interim Dean David Bosco (https://internationalst
 udies.indiana.edu/people/faculty/bosco-david.html) leads a candid\, off-th
 e-record discussion examining how UN political rhetoric translates into ac
 tual policy—and where multilateral governance fractures under the weight
  of major-power politics.What We Will Analyze:The Implementation Gap: How 
 non-binding UNGA resolutions and high-level pledges operate in practice wi
 thout enforcement mechanisms.    Shifting Multilateral Alliances: How emer
 ging powers and voting blocs leverage UN forums to challenge traditional W
 estern influence.    Institutional Limits: The tension between national so
 vereignty\, Security Council dynamics\, and global governance tools.    Ab
 out the Format:Frontlines &amp\; Futures is a structured\, interactive 90-
 minute working salon designed exclusively for MIA\, MIS\, and PhD students
  but all are welcome to join. Rather than a passive lecture\, the session 
 centers on small-group student analysis\, peer debate\, and direct practit
 ioner Q&amp\;A over lunch. Drop in and stay for 30 minutes or the whole ev
 ent.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260903T140147Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2314767-frontlines-futures-the-united-
 nations-general
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-path-to-node="9"><strong data-path-to
 -node="9" data-index-in-node="0">Overview:</strong> Every September\, worl
 d leaders assemble in New York for the United Nations General Assembly to 
 outline strategic priorities and negotiate multilateral commitments. Yet w
 hen the high-level statements wrap up\, international institutions face th
 e hard work of implementation in a system where compliance is largely volu
 ntary.</p><p data-path-to-node="10">  In this edition of <i data-path-to-n
 ode="10" data-index-in-node="19">Frontlines &amp\; Futures</i>\, <a href="
 https://internationalstudies.indiana.edu/people/faculty/bosco-david.html" 
 target="_blank" rel="noopener">Interim Dean David Bosco</a> leads a candid
 \, off-the-record discussion examining how UN political rhetoric translate
 s into actual policy—and where multilateral governance fractures under t
 he weight of major-power politics.</p><p data-path-to-node="11"><strong da
 ta-path-to-node="11" data-index-in-node="0">What We Will Analyze:</strong>
 </p><ul data-path-to-node="12"><li><p data-path-to-node="12\,0\,0"><strong
  data-path-to-node="12\,0\,0" data-index-in-node="0">The Implementation Ga
 p:</strong> How non-binding UNGA resolutions and high-level pledges operat
 e in practice without enforcement mechanisms.    </p></li><li><p data-path
 -to-node="12\,1\,0"><strong data-path-to-node="12\,1\,0" data-index-in-nod
 e="0">Shifting Multilateral Alliances:</strong> How emerging powers and vo
 ting blocs leverage UN forums to challenge traditional Western influence. 
    </p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="12\,2\,0"><strong data-path-to-node=
 "12\,2\,0" data-index-in-node="0">Institutional Limits:</strong> The tensi
 on between national sovereignty\, Security Council dynamics\, and global g
 overnance tools.    </p></li></ul><p data-path-to-node="13"><strong data-p
 ath-to-node="13" data-index-in-node="0">About the Format:</strong><i data-
 path-to-node="13" data-index-in-node="18">Frontlines &amp\; Futures</i> is
  a structured\, interactive 90-minute working salon designed exclusively f
 or MIA\, MIS\, and PhD students but all are welcome to join. Rather than a
  passive lecture\, the session centers on small-group student analysis\, p
 eer debate\, and direct practitioner Q&amp\;A over lunch. Drop in and stay
  for 30 minutes or the whole event.</p>
