BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283986-21jpsi-speaker-series-the-future-of-space-security-i", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283986-21jpsi-speaker-series-the-future-of-space-security-i"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283986-21jpsi-speaker-series-the-future-of-space-security-i"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series "The Future of Space Security in Asia" (Prof
 . Saadia Pekkanen (University of Washington))
DTSTART:20261203T210000Z
DTEND:20261203T221000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153413Z
UID:20261203T210000Z-2402234@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261203T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/162/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,630,810/32140_20261203_21JPSI_SAADIA_PEKKANEN_PNG_Locatio
 n_TBD.rev.1787406878.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space security in Asia by one of 
 the world's leading experts. Hodge Hall (HH) 2079&#160\;(corner of E 10th 
 St and N Fee Lane)  This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, 
 staff\, and members of the community.Please join us!Space has emerged as a
  consequential arena of strategic competition\, defense\, and conflict. As
 ia\, with the greatest concentration of the world's top independent space 
 powers\, is at the forefront of these dynamics. Japan's growing capabiliti
 es\, changing operational postures\, and deepening space collaboration wit
 h the United States are contributing to – and helping shape – this evo
 lving strategic environment\, and broader prospects for war and peace.This
  event is co-sponsored by IU's Cybersecurity and Global Policy Program\, t
 he Department of Political Science\, and the Space Governance Lab.  ——
 ——————————Saadia M. Pekkanen is the Job and Gertrud Ta
 maki Endowed Professor of International Studies\, Adjunct Professor of Pol
 itical Science\, and Adjunct Professor of Law at the University of Washing
 ton in Seattle. She is founding director of the Program on Strategy\, Poli
 cy\, and Diplomacy Research (SPDR) at The Henry M. Jackson School of Inter
 national Studies and the Founding Director of the Space Law\, Data\, and P
 olicy Program (SPACE LDP) at the School of Law. Her research interests are
  in outer space security\, diplomacy\, and international affairs\, with a 
 focus on Japan and Asia. She has published widely\, and is most recently c
 o-editor of the special issue on Space Diplomacy: The Final Frontier of Th
 eory and Practice\, and co-editor of The Oxford Handbook of Space Security
 . She earned Master's degrees from Columbia University and Yale Law School
 \, and a doctorate from Harvard University in political science. She is an
  elected member of the International Institute of Space Law (IISL) and a l
 ifetime member of the Council of Foreign Relations (CFR). For further info
 rmation please visit\, saadiapekkanen.com.Adam LIFF (host/moderator) is Pr
 ofessor of East Asian International Relations at Indiana University's Hami
 lton Lugar School of Global and International Studies\, where he also serv
 es as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Ini
 tiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the
  Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as 
 a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Prog
 ram on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&
 #160\;His research examines contemporary international security and foreig
 n policy challenges in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese pol
 itics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies 
 and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. 
 He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a postgr
 aduate research certificate in International Politics from the University 
 of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford U
 niversity&#160\;All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless otherwise note
 d. This event is made possible thanks to generous support from the Japan F
 oundation.
GEO:39.172366;-86.518375
LAST-MODIFIED:20260901T191341Z
LOCATION:HODGE HALL (KELLEY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS)
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2283986-21jpsi-speaker-series-the-fu
 ture-of-space-security-i
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div id="lw_cal_event_detail_intro" class="lw
 _cal_event_detail_block"><p>    A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space securit
 y in Asia by one of the world's leading experts. <strong>Hodge Hall (HH) 2
 079&#160\;</strong>(corner of E 10th St and N Fee Lane)  </p></div><div id
 ="lw_cal_event_detail_description" class="lw_cal_event_detail_block"><p><e
 m>This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, and member
 s of the community.</em><em>Please join us!</em></p><p><span data-olk-copy
 -source="MailCompose">Space has emerged as a consequential arena of strate
 gic competition\, defense\, and conflict. Asia\, with the greatest concent
 ration of the world's top independent space powers\, is at the forefront o
 f these dynamics. Japan's growing capabilities\, changing operational post
 ures\, and deepening space collaboration with the United States are contri
 buting to – and helping shape – this evolving strategic environment\, 
 and broader prospects for war and peace.</span></p><p><span data-olk-copy-
 source="MailCompose"><em>This event is co-sponsored by IU's Cybersecurity 
 and Global Policy Program\, the Department of Political Science\, and the 
 Space Governance Lab.</em></span></p></div><p>  ————————
 ————</p><div class="profile-about"><div itemprop="description arti
 cleSection"><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose"><strong>Saadia M. 
 Pekkanen</strong> is the Job and Gertrud Tamaki Endowed Professor of Inter
 national Studies\, Adjunct Professor of Political Science\, and Adjunct Pr
 ofessor of Law at the University of Washington in Seattle. She is founding
  director of the Program on Strategy\, Policy\, and Diplomacy Research (SP
 DR) at The Henry M. Jackson School of International Studies and the Foundi
 ng Director of the Space Law\, Data\, and Policy Program (SPACE LDP) at th
 e School of Law. Her research interests are in outer space security\, dipl
 omacy\, and international affairs\, with a focus on Japan and Asia. She ha
 s published widely\, and is most recently co-editor of the special issue o
 n <i>Space Diplomacy: The Final Frontier of Theory and Practice</i>\, and 
 co-editor of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Space Security</i>. She earned Mast
 er's degrees from Columbia University and Yale Law School\, and a doctorat
 e from Harvard University in political science. She is an elected member o
 f the International Institute of Space Law (IISL) and a lifetime member of
  the Council of Foreign Relations (CFR). For further information please vi
 sit\, saadiapekkanen.com.</span></p></div></div><p><strong>Adam LIFF (host
 /moderator)</strong> is Professor of East Asian International Relations at
  Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International St
 udies\, where he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japa
 n Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresid
 ent Senior Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings
  Institution\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at
  Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Insti
 tute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research examines contemporary interna
 tional security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—with a partic
 ular focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan securi
 ty alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.
 S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princ
 eton University\, a postgraduate research certificate in International Pol
 itics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and 
 Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;</p><p><em>All 21JPSI events are
  100% in-person unless otherwise noted. This event is made possible thanks
  to generous support from the Japan Foundation.</em></p>
